{[endif]}

##<center><strong>Contingent Underage Beneficiary Trust in {[if MarriedTF]}{[Spouse.NameCO]}{[endif]}'s Will</strong></center>
{[if MarriedTF]}
{[Spouse.NameCO]}'s Last Will and Testament includes a contingent trust for a minor beneficiary in the event anyone that {[Spouse.Gender.HeShe|else: "he or she"]} names specifically in {[Spouse.Gender.HisHer|else: "his or her"]} Will predeceases {[Spouse.Gender.HimHer|else: "him or her"]} leaving descendants. For example, if {[Spouse.NameCO]} leaves a legacy to a child, and the child predeceases {[Spouse.Gender.HimHer|else: "him or her"]} leaving one or more descendants who are minors, then the assets will be held in trust until such minor descendant(s) of the predeceased child attain eighteen (18) years of age.

The Trustees of each Under-Age Beneficiary's Trust will be the parent, tutor, or legal guardian of that beneficiary.
{[endif]}

{[if MarriedTF]}The Executor(s) are the person or people {[Spouse.NameCO]} wants to manage the distribution of {[Spouse.Gender.HisHer]} estate after {[Spouse.Gender.HeShe]} has passed.{[endif]}

{[if MarriedTF]}{[Spouse.NameCO]} may select Co-Executors (more than one person) to serve at the same time.  Decisions of Co-Executors would be made jointly by mutual consent. People usually choose their spouse as their first and sole initial Executor, but that is not required.{[endif]} 

{[if MarriedTF]}This Will provides for Independent Administration, which means {[Spouse.NameCO]}'s Executors will have the ability to act without pre-approval or permission of a Court.{[endif]}

