DECENDANTS ONLY: This option allows a {[StateLawSelect.GrantorReference]} to add or remove beneficiaries, or modify their rights{[if StateLawSelect == "Louisiana"]} persuant to La. R.S. 9:2031(A){[endif]}. NOTE: This selection does not allow a {[StateLawSelect.GrantorReference]} to make changes that would affect a beneficiary other than a Descendant of a {[StateLawSelect.GrantorReference]}{[if StateLawSelect == "Louisiana"]}, which is in keeping with the provisions of La. R.S. 2031(A){[endif]}. 

ANY BENEFICIARY: his option allows a Settlor to add or remove beneficiaries, or modify their rights regardless of their relationship to the {[StateLawSelect.GrantorReference]}. {[if StateLawSelect == "Louisiana"]}**NOTE: While some practitioners take a more aggressive position, this selection may exceed or at least push the scope permissible principal beneficiary substitutions set forth in La. R.S. 9:2031(A), which are limited to Descendants.**{[endif]}

**ALSO NOTE: The power to add / remove beneficiaries will be removed upon qualification for Medicaid or other need based benefits to minimize the chance of disqualification of the Trust as exclusion as a countable resource.**

SELECTION OPTIONS:

NONE: The power of appointment will terminate for both {[StateLawSelect.GrantorReference]}s upon the death of the first {[StateLawSelect.GrantorReference]};

LIMITED: The power of appoitment will continue for the surviving {[StateLawSelect.GrantorReference]} after the death of the first {[StateLawSelect.GrantorReference]} to die over their own assets, but NOT assets of the first {[StateLawSelect.GrantorReference]} to die; 

EXPANDED: The power of appoitment will continue after the death of the first {[StateLawSelect.GrantorReference]} to die for the surviving {[StateLawSelect.GrantorReference]}, and will include the power to appoint ALL ASSETS of the first {[StateLawSelect.GrantorReference]} to die.

**USE WITH CAUTION: This retained power pertain to the Deceased {[if IsGeauxAppTF]}Settlor{[else]}{[StateLawSelect.GrantorReference]}{[endif]}'s interest in the Trust Assets contained in the Marital Trust.**

DECENDANTS ONLY: This option allows a **surviving** {[if IsGeauxAppTF]}Settlor{[else]}{[StateLawSelect.GrantorReference]}{[endif]} to exercise the power of appointment in a manner that would only affect Descendants of the **surviving** Spouse{[if IsGeauxAppTF || StateLawSelect == "Louisiana"]} pursuant to La. R.S. 9:2031(A){[endif]}. NOTE: This selection does not allow a **surviving** {[if IsGeauxAppTF]}Settlor{[else]}{[StateLawSelect.GrantorReference]}{[endif]} to make changes that would affect a Descendant of the **deceased** {[if IsGeauxAppTF]}Settlor{[else]}{[StateLawSelect.GrantorReference]}{[endif]}{[if IsGeauxAppTF || StateLawSelect == "Louisiana"]}, which is in keeping with the provisions of La. R.S. 2031(A){[endif]}. 

ANY BENEFICIARY: This option allows a **surviving** {[if IsGeauxAppTF]}Settlor{[else]}{[StateLawSelect.GrantorReference]}{[endif]} to exercise the power ofappointment in a manner that would affect **any beneficiary** regardless of their relationship to the surviving {[if IsGeauxAppTF]}Settlor{[else]}{[StateLawSelect.GrantorReference]}{[endif]}. {[if IsGeauxAppTF || StateLawSelect == "Louisiana"]}**NOTE: While some practitioners take a more aggressive position, this selection may exceed or at least push the scope permissible principal beneficiary substitutions set forth in La. R.S. 9:2031(A), which are limited to Descendants of the holder of the power.**{[endif]}

